    "dead-animal-removal": {
      "index": true,
      "metaTitle": "Connecticut Dead Animal Removal | NWCO-Licensed Local Crew Statewide",
      "metaDescription": "Dead Animal removal in Connecticut — our crew removes dead animals, seals entries, and cleans up the mess they leave. NWCO-Licensed, Insured, same-day service available.",
      "h1": "Connecticut Dead Animal Removal",
      "heroSub": "Our crew handles dead animal problems across Connecticut — humane removal, proper sealing, and cleanup — licensed Nuisance Wildlife Control Operator (NWCO), insured, with same-day service and 24/7 availability.",
      "leadParagraph": "<p>Dead Animal problems in Connecticut follow a northern calendar, and where you live in the state changes the job. Connecticut dead-animal calls most often trace to a squirrel or raccoon in a chimney in Hartford and Litchfield county capes and colonials, a mouse or rat in a wall in older New Haven and Bridgeport housing, or an animal under a low deck or in a crawlspace in the shoreline towns. Fall and winter are the peak, when animals that moved in for warmth die in wall voids and attics, but chimney and denning deaths make spring a close second in Connecticut. Older housing helps them — soft fascia, uncapped chimneys, stone foundations, and loosened vents give dead animals openings newer construction does not. Call us and our crew will inspect first, explain what we found plainly, and handle removal and sealing as one job.</p>",
      "extendedBody": "<h2>Where Dead animals Are a Problem in Connecticut</h2>\n<p>Connecticut dead-animal calls most often trace to a squirrel or raccoon in a chimney in Hartford and Litchfield county capes and colonials, a mouse or rat in a wall in older New Haven and Bridgeport housing, or an animal under a low deck or in a crawlspace in the shoreline towns. Lake and vacation homes opened in spring after a quiet winter are a recurring version of the same call — the odor is the first thing that greets the owner at the door.</p>\n<p>Cold Connecticut winters push animals hard toward heated attics and wall voids, while humid summers along Long Island Sound drive a long, busy season in yards, gardens, and shoreline homes. An opening, den, or tunnel system active this month is usually busier next month, so early inspection is the cheapest version of this job, from the Gold Coast, the Connecticut River valley, the Litchfield hills, and the Quiet Corner.</p>\n<h2>Dead Animal Behavior, Damage, and Health Risks in Connecticut</h2>\n<p>Fall and winter are the peak, when animals that moved in for warmth die in wall voids and attics, but chimney and denning deaths make spring a close second in Connecticut.</p>\n<p>The usual call is an odor with no visible cause: something has died in a wall void, attic, crawlspace, chimney, or under a deck or tub, and the smell gets worse for days before it gets better. In these northern states, fall and winter are the peak — animals that moved inside for warmth die in hidden voids — but a raccoon in a chimney in spring or a squirrel in a wall in any season is the same job. Flies gathering at one window or one wall are often the best clue to where the carcass is.</p>\n<p>Finding the animal is most of the work. Our crew uses the odor pattern, fly activity, and the structure itself to narrow the location, and where a wall or ceiling has to be opened, we open as small an area as we can and tell you before we cut. We remove the carcass with protective equipment — carcasses of rabies-vector animals are handled with particular care and never bare-handed — then disinfect and deodorize the cavity, and replace fouled insulation where it has absorbed the odor. Just as important, we find how the animal got in, because a dead animal indoors almost always means a live-animal entry point that is still open.</p>\n<h2>Connecticut Rules for Dead Animal Work</h2>\n<p>There is no special Connecticut license barrier to picking up a carcass, and we will not invent one. What does apply is rabies care: Connecticut's rabies-vector animals — raccoon, skunk, and fox — are handled with protective equipment and never bare-handed, and any pet contact with a carcass should be reported to your veterinarian the same day.</p>\n<p>We hold a Connecticut NWCO license issued by DEEP and follow DEEP rules on every job. We are insured, we offer same-day service when a call cannot wait, and we are available 24/7. If a job falls outside what Connecticut allows, we will tell you on the first call and point you to CT DEEP.</p>\n<h2>What a Connecticut Dead Animal Removal Job Includes</h2>\n<ul class=\"wildlife-list\">\n  <li><strong>Location work</strong> — narrowing the carcass location from odor, flies, and building layout before anything is opened.</li>\n  <li><strong>Removal</strong> — retrieving the carcass from attics, walls, crawlspaces, chimneys, and under structures, with protective equipment.</li>\n  <li><strong>Disinfection and deodorizing</strong> — treating the cavity or area and removing insulation that has absorbed the odor.</li>\n  <li><strong>Small, honest openings</strong> — cutting only what is needed to reach the animal, and telling you before we open a wall or ceiling.</li>\n  <li><strong>Entry-point check</strong> — finding and sealing how the animal got inside, so the next one does not die in the same place.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every job ends the same way: the animals are out, the openings or burrows are closed, the mess they left is cleaned up, and you know exactly what we found and what we did. That finish — not a trap checked once and a hole left open — is what keeps dead animals from becoming a twice-a-year expense in Connecticut.</p>",
      "faqs": [
        {
          "q": "How long will a dead animal smell in a Connecticut house?",
          "a": "In a heated house, odor usually builds for several days, peaks, and can linger for weeks in a wall void if the carcass stays there, especially in winter when houses are closed up tight. Removing the animal and the fouled insulation is what shortens it — masking sprays do not."
        },
        {
          "q": "What are the Connecticut rules for dead animal removal?",
          "a": "There is no special Connecticut license barrier to picking up a carcass, and we will not invent one. What does apply is rabies care: Connecticut's rabies-vector animals — raccoon, skunk, and fox — are handled with protective equipment and never bare-handed, and any pet contact with a carcass should be reported to your veterinarian the same day."
        },
        {
          "q": "How much does dead animal removal cost in Connecticut?",
          "a": "It depends on the job. A straightforward dead animal job costs less than one that needs full exclusion, cleanup, decontamination, and repair, and typical ranges vary by how many animals are present, how many entry points we have to seal, and how much damage they have done. We quote after we inspect your home, so you see exactly what we found before you decide."
        }
      ]
    }
