      "leadParagraph": "<p>Bat problems in New Jersey follow the housing and the landscape. In the northeast corridor — Bergen, Essex, Hudson, Union, Passaic and Middlesex — dense older housing produces the state's rat, squirrel and raccoon volume. Our crew inspects first, explains what we found plainly, and handles removal and sealing as one job — call us at (844) 544-3498 and we will talk through what you are seeing.</p>",
      "extendedBody": "<h2>Where Bats Are a Problem in New Jersey</h2>\n<p>In the northeast corridor — Bergen, Essex, Hudson, Union, Passaic and Middlesex — dense older housing produces the state's rat, squirrel and raccoon volume. The Skylands and Highlands of Sussex, Warren, Morris and Hunterdon are rocky and forested, with the state's snake country and bat hibernacula in Morris County mines. The Pine Barrens across Burlington, Ocean and Atlantic hold sandy-soil burrowing animals and a southern timber rattlesnake population, and the Shore counties — Monmouth, Ocean, Atlantic and Cape May — add the empty-off-season-house problem that defines so much New Jersey work.</p>\n<h2>Bat Behavior, Damage, and Health Risks in New Jersey</h2>\n<p>Bats enter through gaps as small as a pencil line at rooflines, ridge vents and chimney flashing, then roost where it is warm and undisturbed. Colonies return to the same roost year after year, and young that cannot yet fly are present through the maternity season.</p>\n<p>Guano accumulates under roosts and can stain ceilings and carry odor through a house in summer. Bats themselves are beneficial and protected — the job is never to harm them, it is to give them a one-way exit and seal the building behind them.</p>\n<h2>New Jersey Rules for Bat Work</h2>\n<p>Every native bat in New Jersey is protected under the Endangered and Nongame Species Conservation Act, and five of the state's nine species — including the little brown, northern long-eared and Indiana bats — are state-Endangered, so bat work here is exclusion only, never lethal and never relocation. We work inside NJ Fish & Wildlife's Nuisance Wildlife Control Guidelines for Bats: evictions only from April 1 to May 14 and from August 1 to October 15, no evictions from May 15 to July 31 while flightless pups are in the roost, and extra care from October 16 to March 31 for bats hibernating in buildings. Those windows are Division guidelines backed by ENSCA, and one-way excluders stay in place at least a week, including three consecutive nights above 50 degrees, before we seal permanently. Call us at (844) 544-3498 to get an inspection booked ahead of a window.</p>\n<p>We are insured, we offer same-day service when a call cannot wait, and we are available 24/7. If a job falls outside what New Jersey allows, we will tell you on the first call and point you to NJ Fish & Wildlife.</p>\n<h2>What a New Jersey Bat Job Includes</h2>\n<ul class=\\\"wildlife-list\\\">\n  <li><strong>Inspection</strong> — locating every entry point, den or active run, and checking for dependent young before any work is planned.</li>\n  <li><strong>Humane handling</strong> — live trapping, one-way exclusion or non-lethal capture suited to the species and the site, using cage and box traps at homes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Exclusion</strong> — sealing soffits, vents, foundation gaps and den openings with materials the animal cannot chew or dig through.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cleanup</strong> — removing nesting material, droppings and contaminated insulation, then sanitizing the space.</li>\n  <li><strong>Prevention</strong> — a walk-through of the attractants and openings that caused the problem, so it does not refill next season.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every job ends the same way: the animals are out, the openings are closed, the mess is cleaned up, and you know exactly what we found and what we did. Call us at (844) 544-3498 to book an inspection anywhere in New Jersey.</p>",
