      "leadParagraph": "<p>Snake problems in New Jersey follow the housing and the landscape. In the northeast corridor — Bergen, Essex, Hudson, Union, Passaic and Middlesex — dense older housing produces the state's rat, squirrel and raccoon volume. Our crew inspects first, explains what we found plainly, and handles removal and sealing as one job — call us at (844) 544-3498 and we will talk through what you are seeing.</p>",
      "extendedBody": "<h2>Where Snakes Are a Problem in New Jersey</h2>\n<p>In the northeast corridor — Bergen, Essex, Hudson, Union, Passaic and Middlesex — dense older housing produces the state's rat, squirrel and raccoon volume. The Skylands and Highlands of Sussex, Warren, Morris and Hunterdon are rocky and forested, with the state's snake country and bat hibernacula in Morris County mines. The Pine Barrens across Burlington, Ocean and Atlantic hold sandy-soil burrowing animals and a southern timber rattlesnake population, and the Shore counties — Monmouth, Ocean, Atlantic and Cape May — add the empty-off-season-house problem that defines so much New Jersey work.</p>\n<h2>Snake Behavior, Damage, and Health Risks in New Jersey</h2>\n<p>Most snakes found in a home are harmless species hunting mice in basements, crawlspaces and stone foundations. They enter through foundation cracks, bulkhead doors and gaps around pipes, and a snake indoors usually means rodents and an opening — both worth fixing.</p>\n<p>A calm identification comes first. Color and pattern alone mislead people constantly — harmless watersnakes and milksnakes are routinely mistaken for venomous species — so our crew confirms what the animal is before anyone decides what happens next.</p>\n<h2>New Jersey Rules for Snake Work</h2>\n<p>New Jersey snake work starts with a rule that shapes everything: under the Endangered and Nongame Species Conservation Act it is illegal to kill, harm, harass or collect any native nongame snake. The state has 22 snake species and only two venomous ones — the timber rattlesnake, state-Endangered, and the northern copperhead, state-Threatened under the January 2025 list. A venomous snake on your property is a safety and referral job: keep people and pets away and it goes to NJ Fish & Wildlife's Venomous Snake Response Team, whose trained volunteers relocate venomous snakes on request. Our crew's own snake work is identification, reassurance, and — for a non-listed, non-venomous snake inside a home — non-lethal catch and outdoor release within about 200 meters, the maximum the Division's relocation policy allows for snakes. We never promise venomous snake removal and there are no native cottonmouths, diamondbacks or coral snakes in New Jersey to fear.</p>\n<p>We are insured, we offer same-day service when a call cannot wait, and we are available 24/7. If a job falls outside what New Jersey allows, we will tell you on the first call and point you to NJ Fish & Wildlife.</p>\n<h2>What a New Jersey Snake Job Includes</h2>\n<ul class=\\\"wildlife-list\\\">\n  <li><strong>Inspection</strong> — locating every entry point, den or active run, and checking for dependent young before any work is planned.</li>\n  <li><strong>Humane handling</strong> — live trapping, one-way exclusion or non-lethal capture suited to the species and the site, using cage and box traps at homes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Exclusion</strong> — sealing soffits, vents, foundation gaps and den openings with materials the animal cannot chew or dig through.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cleanup</strong> — removing nesting material, droppings and contaminated insulation, then sanitizing the space.</li>\n  <li><strong>Prevention</strong> — a walk-through of the attractants and openings that caused the problem, so it does not refill next season.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every job ends the same way: the animals are out, the openings are closed, the mess is cleaned up, and you know exactly what we found and what we did. Call us at (844) 544-3498 to book an inspection anywhere in New Jersey.</p>",
