      "leadParagraph": "<p>Skunk problems in New Jersey follow the housing and the landscape. In the northeast corridor — Bergen, Essex, Hudson, Union, Passaic and Middlesex — dense older housing produces the state's rat, squirrel and raccoon volume. Our crew inspects first, explains what we found plainly, and handles removal and sealing as one job — call us at (844) 544-3498 and we will talk through what you are seeing.</p>",
      "extendedBody": "<h2>Where Skunks Are a Problem in New Jersey</h2>\n<p>In the northeast corridor — Bergen, Essex, Hudson, Union, Passaic and Middlesex — dense older housing produces the state's rat, squirrel and raccoon volume. The Skylands and Highlands of Sussex, Warren, Morris and Hunterdon are rocky and forested, with the state's snake country and bat hibernacula in Morris County mines. The Pine Barrens across Burlington, Ocean and Atlantic hold sandy-soil burrowing animals and a southern timber rattlesnake population, and the Shore counties — Monmouth, Ocean, Atlantic and Cape May — add the empty-off-season-house problem that defines so much New Jersey work.</p>\n<h2>Skunk Behavior, Damage, and Health Risks in New Jersey</h2>\n<p>Striped skunks den under decks, porches, sheds and crawlspaces, and dig small cone-shaped holes in lawns hunting grubs at night. Late winter is mating season, when smell complaints spike even without a den on the property.</p>\n<p>A skunk under a porch announces itself at dusk. The work is removal without a spraying incident — approach, cover and timing matter — followed by screening the den opening so the next skunk does not move into a ready-made home.</p>\n<h2>New Jersey Rules for Skunk Work</h2>\n<p>Skunks are named in the Game Code at N.J.A.C. 7:25-5.21 — a property owner, or their agent designated in writing, may control them at any time by lawful procedures, and no New Jersey wildlife license exists for the work. The skunk is also one of the state's five rabies-vector species on the Department of Health list, which drives both our handling and the relocation rules: NJ Fish & Wildlife policy prefers on-site release after the den is screened off, and an adult skunk that must be moved stays within its township of origin, no more than 5 miles from capture, with the release landowner's permission. Our crew uses cage and box live traps and covered handling — steel-jaw leghold traps have been banned outright in New Jersey since 1985, and we would not use one if they were not.</p>\n<p>We are insured, we offer same-day service when a call cannot wait, and we are available 24/7. If a job falls outside what New Jersey allows, we will tell you on the first call and point you to NJ Fish & Wildlife.</p>\n<h2>What a New Jersey Skunk Job Includes</h2>\n<ul class=\\\"wildlife-list\\\">\n  <li><strong>Inspection</strong> — locating every entry point, den or active run, and checking for dependent young before any work is planned.</li>\n  <li><strong>Humane handling</strong> — live trapping, one-way exclusion or non-lethal capture suited to the species and the site, using cage and box traps at homes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Exclusion</strong> — sealing soffits, vents, foundation gaps and den openings with materials the animal cannot chew or dig through.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cleanup</strong> — removing nesting material, droppings and contaminated insulation, then sanitizing the space.</li>\n  <li><strong>Prevention</strong> — a walk-through of the attractants and openings that caused the problem, so it does not refill next season.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every job ends the same way: the animals are out, the openings are closed, the mess is cleaned up, and you know exactly what we found and what we did. Call us at (844) 544-3498 to book an inspection anywhere in New Jersey.</p>",
