      "metaTitle": "New Jersey Opossum Removal | Local Crew Statewide",
      "metaDescription": "Opossum removal in New Jersey — our crew handles the animals, seals the entries, and cleans up the mess they leave. Insured, same-day service available. Call us at (844) 544-3498.",
      "h1": "New Jersey Opossum Removal",
      "heroSub": "Our crew handles opossum problems across New Jersey — humane handling, proper sealing, and cleanup — insured, with same-day service and 24/7 availability.",
      "leadParagraph": "<p>Opossum problems in New Jersey follow the housing and the landscape. In the northeast corridor — Bergen, Essex, Hudson, Union, Passaic and Middlesex — dense older housing produces the state's rat, squirrel and raccoon volume. Our crew inspects first, explains what we found plainly, and handles removal and sealing as one job — call us at (844) 544-3498 and we will talk through what you are seeing.</p>",
      "extendedBody": "<h2>Where Opossums Are a Problem in New Jersey</h2>\n<p>In the northeast corridor — Bergen, Essex, Hudson, Union, Passaic and Middlesex — dense older housing produces the state's rat, squirrel and raccoon volume. The Skylands and Highlands of Sussex, Warren, Morris and Hunterdon are rocky and forested, with the state's snake country and bat hibernacula in Morris County mines. The Pine Barrens across Burlington, Ocean and Atlantic hold sandy-soil burrowing animals and a southern timber rattlesnake population, and the Shore counties — Monmouth, Ocean, Atlantic and Cape May — add the empty-off-season-house problem that defines so much New Jersey work.</p>\n<h2>Opossum Behavior, Damage, and Health Risks in New Jersey</h2>\n<p>Virginia opossums are solitary wanderers that den under decks and sheds, in crawlspaces and occasionally in attics, and they rarely stay long unless food and cover are steady. They are not aggressive and play dead rather than fight.</p>\n<p>Opossums tear duct insulation and leave droppings in crawlspaces, and they draw attention mostly because dogs find them at night. They are not a rabies concern in the way raccoons and skunks are, and the job is usually removal plus closing the den opening.</p>\n<h2>New Jersey Rules for Opossum Work</h2>\n<p>Opossums are named in the Game Code at N.J.A.C. 7:25-5.21, so the work runs on the property-owner's-agent authority — designated in writing — rather than any state wildlife license, which New Jersey does not issue. Opossums are not on either New Jersey rabies list, and our copy and our crew treat them accordingly: no rabies framing, just humane removal and closing the den. NJ Fish & Wildlife's relocation policy prefers release close to the capture site, within about two miles for mammals generally and always with the landowner's permission, and no release ever happens on public land without prior permission.</p>\n<p>We are insured, we offer same-day service when a call cannot wait, and we are available 24/7. If a job falls outside what New Jersey allows, we will tell you on the first call and point you to NJ Fish & Wildlife.</p>\n<h2>What a New Jersey Opossum Job Includes</h2>\n<ul class=\\\"wildlife-list\\\">\n  <li><strong>Inspection</strong> — locating every entry point, den or active run, and checking for dependent young before any work is planned.</li>\n  <li><strong>Humane handling</strong> — live trapping, one-way exclusion or non-lethal capture suited to the species and the site, using cage and box traps at homes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Exclusion</strong> — sealing soffits, vents, foundation gaps and den openings with materials the animal cannot chew or dig through.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cleanup</strong> — removing nesting material, droppings and contaminated insulation, then sanitizing the space.</li>\n  <li><strong>Prevention</strong> — a walk-through of the attractants and openings that caused the problem, so it does not refill next season.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every job ends the same way: the animals are out, the openings are closed, the mess is cleaned up, and you know exactly what we found and what we did. Call us at (844) 544-3498 to book an inspection anywhere in New Jersey.</p>",
