      "leadParagraph": "<p>Dead Animal problems in New Jersey follow the housing and the landscape. In the northeast corridor — Bergen, Essex, Hudson, Union, Passaic and Middlesex — dense older housing produces the state's rat, squirrel and raccoon volume. Our crew inspects first, explains what we found plainly, and handles removal and sealing as one job — call us at (844) 544-3498 and we will talk through what you are seeing.</p>",
      "extendedBody": "<h2>Where Dead Animals Are a Problem in New Jersey</h2>\n<p>In the northeast corridor — Bergen, Essex, Hudson, Union, Passaic and Middlesex — dense older housing produces the state's rat, squirrel and raccoon volume. The Skylands and Highlands of Sussex, Warren, Morris and Hunterdon are rocky and forested, with the state's snake country and bat hibernacula in Morris County mines. The Pine Barrens across Burlington, Ocean and Atlantic hold sandy-soil burrowing animals and a southern timber rattlesnake population, and the Shore counties — Monmouth, Ocean, Atlantic and Cape May — add the empty-off-season-house problem that defines so much New Jersey work.</p>\n<h2>Dead Animal Behavior, Damage, and Health Risks in New Jersey</h2>\n<p>Animals die in wall voids, chimneys, crawlspaces and under decks after sickness, injury or a failed entry, and the odor peaks in warm weather. A carcass in a wall is a location problem first: finding exactly where it is decides whether the fix is simple or involves opening a small section of wall.</p>\n<p>Our crew locates and removes the carcass, disinfects the area, and closes the entry the animal used, because a dead-animal call without sealing is usually the first of several. Carcasses of rabies-vector species are handled with rabies precautions and never with bare hands.</p>\n<h2>New Jersey Rules for Dead Animal Work</h2>\n<p>Dead-animal pickup in New Jersey carries no licensing barrier, and our crew treats the rabies-vector carcasses — raccoon, skunk, fox, groundhog and bat — with rabies precautions throughout. A deer carcass on a road is a municipal or state matter rather than a booking, and we will tell you that on the call instead of charging you for a trip we cannot complete. For a carcass on your property or inside the structure, call us at (844) 544-3498 and describe where the odor is strongest; that detail is what lets us find it on the first visit.</p>\n<p>We are insured, we offer same-day service when a call cannot wait, and we are available 24/7. If a job falls outside what New Jersey allows, we will tell you on the first call and point you to NJ Fish & Wildlife.</p>\n<h2>What a New Jersey Dead Animal Job Includes</h2>\n<ul class=\\\"wildlife-list\\\">\n  <li><strong>Inspection</strong> — locating every entry point, den or active run, and checking for dependent young before any work is planned.</li>\n  <li><strong>Humane handling</strong> — live trapping, one-way exclusion or non-lethal capture suited to the species and the site, using cage and box traps at homes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Exclusion</strong> — sealing soffits, vents, foundation gaps and den openings with materials the animal cannot chew or dig through.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cleanup</strong> — removing nesting material, droppings and contaminated insulation, then sanitizing the space.</li>\n  <li><strong>Prevention</strong> — a walk-through of the attractants and openings that caused the problem, so it does not refill next season.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every job ends the same way: the animals are out, the openings are closed, the mess is cleaned up, and you know exactly what we found and what we did. Call us at (844) 544-3498 to book an inspection anywhere in New Jersey.</p>",
