      "heroSub": "Our crew handles squirrel problems across Maryland — humane handling, proper sealing, and cleanup — insured, with same-day service and 24/7 availability.",
      "leadParagraph": "<p>Squirrel problems in Maryland follow the housing and the landscape. Western Maryland — Garrett, Allegany and Washington counties — is mountain and valley country with the state's timber rattlesnake range and cold-winter attic pressure. Our crew inspects first, explains what we found plainly, and handles removal and sealing as one job — call us at (844) 544-3498 and we will talk through what you are seeing.</p>",
      "extendedBody": "<h2>Where Squirrels Are a Problem in Maryland</h2>\n<p>Western Maryland — Garrett, Allegany and Washington counties — is mountain and valley country with the state's timber rattlesnake range and cold-winter attic pressure. The Piedmont and the Baltimore-Washington corridor produce the heaviest raccoon, squirrel and rat volume in Maryland's rowhouse and suburb stock. The Eastern Shore's nine Delmarva counties are waterfront and farm country, where raised houses, crawlspaces and mature pine stands shape the call list, and southern Maryland's peninsulas mix naval and exurban growth with tobacco-farm groundhog country.</p>\n<h2>Squirrel Behavior, Damage, and Health Risks in Maryland</h2>\n<p>Gray squirrels are the usual attic animal, breeding twice a year, so timing matters: sealing while a female nurses hidden young walls them inside. Daytime running and rolling sounds usually mean grays; scratching only after dark can mean flying squirrels.</p>\n<p>Squirrels gnaw constantly to wear down ever-growing teeth — wood, stored items and electrical wiring included, a genuine fire concern in older housing. They shred insulation for nests and can widen a finger-width soffit gap into a doorway in days.</p>\n<h2>Maryland Rules for Squirrel Work</h2>\n<p>Gray squirrels — along with eastern fox, red and southern flying squirrels — are on the codified Wildlife Damage Control Permit species list at COMAR 08.03.15.08, and our crew does this work as Maryland DNR-licensed Wildlife Damage Control Operators. Squirrels are not a rabies story in Maryland; the Department of Health notes rabies is rarely reported in small rodents. Where a trapped squirrel is released off site, COMAR 08.03.15.24 sets the terms: a wildlife management area or another area with written permission from the landowner, within the same or an adjacent county. Our standard job remains one-way exclusion and steel sealing, which solves the problem where it started.</p>\n<p>We are insured, we offer same-day service when a call cannot wait, and we are available 24/7. If a job falls outside what COMAR 08.03.15 allows, we will tell you on the first call and point you to the Maryland DNR Wildlife and Heritage Service.</p>\n<h2>What a Maryland Squirrel Job Includes</h2>\n<ul class=\\\"wildlife-list\\\">\n  <li><strong>Inspection</strong> — locating every entry point, den or active run, and checking for dependent young before any work is planned.</li>\n  <li><strong>Humane handling</strong> — live trapping, one-way exclusion or non-lethal capture suited to the species and the site, using cage and box traps at homes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Exclusion</strong> — sealing soffits, vents, foundation gaps and den openings with materials the animal cannot chew or dig through.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cleanup</strong> — removing nesting material, droppings and contaminated insulation, then sanitizing the space.</li>\n  <li><strong>Prevention</strong> — a walk-through of the attractants and openings that caused the problem, so it does not refill next season.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every job ends the same way: the animals are out, the openings are closed, the mess is cleaned up, and you know exactly what we found and what we did. Call us at (844) 544-3498 to book an inspection anywhere in Maryland.</p>",
