      "leadParagraph": "<p>Bat problems in Maryland follow the housing and the landscape. Western Maryland — Garrett, Allegany and Washington counties — is mountain and valley country with the state's timber rattlesnake range and cold-winter attic pressure. Our crew inspects first, explains what we found plainly, and handles removal and sealing as one job — call us at (844) 544-3498 and we will talk through what you are seeing.</p>",
      "extendedBody": "<h2>Where Bats Are a Problem in Maryland</h2>\n<p>Western Maryland — Garrett, Allegany and Washington counties — is mountain and valley country with the state's timber rattlesnake range and cold-winter attic pressure. The Piedmont and the Baltimore-Washington corridor produce the heaviest raccoon, squirrel and rat volume in Maryland's rowhouse and suburb stock. The Eastern Shore's nine Delmarva counties are waterfront and farm country, where raised houses, crawlspaces and mature pine stands shape the call list, and southern Maryland's peninsulas mix naval and exurban growth with tobacco-farm groundhog country.</p>\n<h2>Bat Behavior, Damage, and Health Risks in Maryland</h2>\n<p>Bats enter through gaps as small as a pencil line at rooflines, ridge vents and chimney flashing, then roost where it is warm and undisturbed. Colonies return to the same roost year after year, and young that cannot yet fly are present through the maternity season.</p>\n<p>Guano accumulates under roosts and can stain ceilings and carry odor through a house in summer. Bats themselves are beneficial and protected — the job is never to harm them, it is to give them a one-way exit and seal the building behind them.</p>\n<h2>Maryland Rules for Bat Work</h2>\n<p>Maryland bat work is non-lethal by regulation, full stop. COMAR 08.03.15.20 requires a wildlife damage control operator to remove bats only by nonlethal methods, and our permit specifically covers mammals including bats, as Maryland DNR policy requires for any company doing nuisance bat work. Two timing rules apply and they are different kinds of rule: by regulation, a colony of more than 10 adults needs prior approval from the Wildlife and Heritage Service before exclusion between March 1 and August 31; as DNR guidance, we recommend and plan exclusions for September 1 to March 1, provided bats are not hibernating in the building, and May and June — flightless pup season — is when exclusion is not recommended. Three Maryland bats are state-Endangered, including the Indiana and northern long-eared bats, and no permit we hold authorizes work on a listed species. Call us at (844) 544-3498 and we will size the colony and set the lawful plan.</p>\n<p>We are insured, we offer same-day service when a call cannot wait, and we are available 24/7. If a job falls outside what COMAR 08.03.15 allows, we will tell you on the first call and point you to the Maryland DNR Wildlife and Heritage Service.</p>\n<h2>What a Maryland Bat Job Includes</h2>\n<ul class=\\\"wildlife-list\\\">\n  <li><strong>Inspection</strong> — locating every entry point, den or active run, and checking for dependent young before any work is planned.</li>\n  <li><strong>Humane handling</strong> — live trapping, one-way exclusion or non-lethal capture suited to the species and the site, using cage and box traps at homes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Exclusion</strong> — sealing soffits, vents, foundation gaps and den openings with materials the animal cannot chew or dig through.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cleanup</strong> — removing nesting material, droppings and contaminated insulation, then sanitizing the space.</li>\n  <li><strong>Prevention</strong> — a walk-through of the attractants and openings that caused the problem, so it does not refill next season.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every job ends the same way: the animals are out, the openings are closed, the mess is cleaned up, and you know exactly what we found and what we did. Call us at (844) 544-3498 to book an inspection anywhere in Maryland.</p>",
