      "leadParagraph": "<p>Groundhog problems in Maryland follow the housing and the landscape. Western Maryland — Garrett, Allegany and Washington counties — is mountain and valley country with the state's timber rattlesnake range and cold-winter attic pressure. Our crew inspects first, explains what we found plainly, and handles removal and sealing as one job — call us at (844) 544-3498 and we will talk through what you are seeing.</p>",
      "extendedBody": "<h2>Where Groundhogs Are a Problem in Maryland</h2>\n<p>Western Maryland — Garrett, Allegany and Washington counties — is mountain and valley country with the state's timber rattlesnake range and cold-winter attic pressure. The Piedmont and the Baltimore-Washington corridor produce the heaviest raccoon, squirrel and rat volume in Maryland's rowhouse and suburb stock. The Eastern Shore's nine Delmarva counties are waterfront and farm country, where raised houses, crawlspaces and mature pine stands shape the call list, and southern Maryland's peninsulas mix naval and exurban growth with tobacco-farm groundhog country.</p>\n<h2>Groundhog Behavior, Damage, and Health Risks in Maryland</h2>\n<p>Groundhogs — woodchucks — dig burrow systems with several entrances, often under decks, sheds, porches and foundation edges, and they feed heavily in gardens and landscaped beds through spring and summer before hibernating in winter.</p>\n<p>Burrows undermine slabs, walkways and retaining walls, and a burrow under a foundation can channel water against the house. Look for crescent-shaped dirt fans at entrances and clean-clipped garden damage.</p>\n<h2>Maryland Rules for Groundhog Work</h2>\n<p>Woodchucks are on the Wildlife Damage Control Permit species list at COMAR 08.03.15.08, and our crew handles them as Maryland DNR-licensed Wildlife Damage Control Operators; landowners, by contrast, need no permit for a groundhog damaging their own property. On rabies, Maryland's two official lists differ and we keep them straight: the COMAR rabies-vector definition names bats, foxes, raccoons and skunks — not groundhogs — while the Maryland Department of Health lists groundhogs among the animals rabies is most often found in, so our caution with a strange-acting groundhog comes from the Health Department's data, not a claim about the regulation. Released groundhogs follow COMAR 08.03.15.24: on site, or within the same or an adjacent county with written release-site permission.</p>\n<p>We are insured, we offer same-day service when a call cannot wait, and we are available 24/7. If a job falls outside what COMAR 08.03.15 allows, we will tell you on the first call and point you to the Maryland DNR Wildlife and Heritage Service.</p>\n<h2>What a Maryland Groundhog Job Includes</h2>\n<ul class=\\\"wildlife-list\\\">\n  <li><strong>Inspection</strong> — locating every entry point, den or active run, and checking for dependent young before any work is planned.</li>\n  <li><strong>Humane handling</strong> — live trapping, one-way exclusion or non-lethal capture suited to the species and the site, using cage and box traps at homes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Exclusion</strong> — sealing soffits, vents, foundation gaps and den openings with materials the animal cannot chew or dig through.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cleanup</strong> — removing nesting material, droppings and contaminated insulation, then sanitizing the space.</li>\n  <li><strong>Prevention</strong> — a walk-through of the attractants and openings that caused the problem, so it does not refill next season.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every job ends the same way: the animals are out, the openings are closed, the mess is cleaned up, and you know exactly what we found and what we did. Call us at (844) 544-3498 to book an inspection anywhere in Maryland.</p>",
