      "extendedBody": "<h2>Where Dead Animals Are a Problem in Maryland</h2>\n<p>Western Maryland — Garrett, Allegany and Washington counties — is mountain and valley country with the state's timber rattlesnake range and cold-winter attic pressure. The Piedmont and the Baltimore-Washington corridor produce the heaviest raccoon, squirrel and rat volume in Maryland's rowhouse and suburb stock. The Eastern Shore's nine Delmarva counties are waterfront and farm country, where raised houses, crawlspaces and mature pine stands shape the call list, and southern Maryland's peninsulas mix naval and exurban growth with tobacco-farm groundhog country.</p>\n<h2>Dead Animal Behavior, Damage, and Health Risks in Maryland</h2>\n<p>Animals die in wall voids, chimneys, crawlspaces and under decks after sickness, injury or a failed entry, and the odor peaks in warm weather. A carcass in a wall is a location problem first: finding exactly where it is decides whether the fix is simple or involves opening a small section of wall.</p>\n<p>Our crew locates and removes the carcass, disinfects the area, and closes the entry the animal used, because a dead-animal call without sealing is usually the first of several. Carcasses of rabies-vector species are handled with rabies precautions and never with bare hands.</p>\n<h2>Maryland Rules for Dead Animal Work</h2>\n<p>Dead-animal removal in Maryland is a service we offer straightforwardly: COMAR mentions private carcass work only in passing, and no specific license governs a homeowner pickup, so we do not dress the job up in a credential it does not need. Rabies precautions apply throughout to raccoon, skunk, fox and bat carcasses — Maryland's codified rabies vectors — and our crew bags, removes and disinfects without bare-hand contact. Call us at (844) 544-3498 and tell us where the odor is strongest and how long it has been there; that is what lets us locate a carcass in a wall or crawlspace on the first visit.</p>\n<p>We are insured, we offer same-day service when a call cannot wait, and we are available 24/7. If a job falls outside what COMAR 08.03.15 allows, we will tell you on the first call and point you to the Maryland DNR Wildlife and Heritage Service.</p>\n<h2>What a Maryland Dead Animal Job Includes</h2>\n<ul class=\\\"wildlife-list\\\">\n  <li><strong>Inspection</strong> — locating every entry point, den or active run, and checking for dependent young before any work is planned.</li>\n  <li><strong>Humane handling</strong> — live trapping, one-way exclusion or non-lethal capture suited to the species and the site, using cage and box traps at homes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Exclusion</strong> — sealing soffits, vents, foundation gaps and den openings with materials the animal cannot chew or dig through.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cleanup</strong> — removing nesting material, droppings and contaminated insulation, then sanitizing the space.</li>\n  <li><strong>Prevention</strong> — a walk-through of the attractants and openings that caused the problem, so it does not refill next season.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every job ends the same way: the animals are out, the openings are closed, the mess is cleaned up, and you know exactly what we found and what we did. Call us at (844) 544-3498 to book an inspection anywhere in Maryland.</p>",
