      "leadParagraph": "<p>The eastern mole occurs throughout Florida, tunnelling just under the surface of lawns, beds, and pastures hunting earthworms and grubs, and pushing up the raised ridges and small molehills homeowners find after rain. Florida's sandy soils make mole sign easy to see and easy to confuse: a southeastern pocket gopher in north and central Florida leaves a row of larger sand mounds, an armadillo leaves scattered surface digging, and a gopher tortoise leaves a burrow that must never be touched. Identification is the first step of every Florida mole call.</p>",
      "extendedBody": "<h2>Where Moles Are a Problem in Florida</h2>\n<p>Irrigated lawns, golf-course and retirement-community turf, horse paddocks, cemeteries, and any sandy ground kept moist and full of earthworms — which is most Florida suburbs in the rainy season. Surface tunnels collapse underfoot, roots are exposed in beds, and mowing over ridges dulls blades and scalps turf. Mole activity surges after rain, when worms move up and the ground digs easily.</p>\n<h2>Mole Behavior and Damage in Florida</h2>\n<p>Eastern moles are solitary, nearly blind, and active day and night in bouts, maintaining a deeper permanent tunnel system with shallow feeding runs branching off it. Trapping only works in active runs, so our crew probes and marks runs and returns to check which are being reused — setting traps in abandoned tunnels is the most common wasted effort on mole jobs. Moles themselves do not eat plants; the damage is the tunnelling and the animals that use their runs.</p>\n<h2>Florida Rules and Identification Boundaries</h2>\n<p>A large burrow entrance with a wide, flat, half-moon apron of sand is a gopher tortoise burrow until proven otherwise: the tortoise is State Threatened, a permit is required for work within 25 feet of a burrow, and only an FWC Authorized Agent may relocate one — our crew leaves it alone and tells you who to call. Pocket gopher mounds are a different animal and a different plan, which we will name at inspection. Work is done under FWC Rule 68A-9.010 with the property owner's authorization — Florida issues no nuisance-trapper license.</p>\n<h2>What a Florida Mole Job Includes</h2>\n<p>Identification of the digging — mole, pocket gopher, armadillo, or protected tortoise burrow — probing and marking of active runs, trapping in those runs with follow-up checks, and advice on irrigation and grub pressure that is making the lawn attractive.</p>\n",
