      "leadParagraph": "<p>The nine-banded armadillo is naturalised and common throughout most of Florida — a non-protected mammal that may be taken year-round, and one of the state's most persistent yard diggers. Armadillos root through lawns and flower beds at night for grubs and earthworms, dig burrows under slabs, decks, and air-conditioning pads, and can undermine walkways and pool decks. Florida's Department of Health lists armadillos as low-risk for rabies, so armadillo copy here carries no rabies framing — the problem is digging, full stop.</p>",
      "extendedBody": "<h2>Where Armadillos Are a Problem in Florida</h2>\n<p>Sandy, diggable ground near water and woods — canal and lake suburbs, golf communities, horse country, and rural homes statewide. Burrows appear under structures where the soil is soft and shaded, and lawn damage is worst in irrigated yards full of grubs after rain. A yard can go from clean to dug up in a single night during peak grub season.</p>\n<h2>Armadillo Behavior — and Honest Trapping Expectations</h2>\n<p>Armadillos have a strong sense of smell, poor eyesight, and set nightly routes between burrow and feeding ground. FWC itself notes they are difficult to capture in live traps, and we repeat that honestly: trapping works best by placing traps along fences, walls, and burrow entrances where the animal already travels, using barriers to guide it in, rather than relying on bait. Exclusion — burying fencing along beds and screening structure gaps — is often the durable half of the job. Wear gloves around burrows and digging, and keep dogs from cornering one.</p>\n<h2>Florida Rules for Armadillo Release — the Correction That Matters</h2>\n<p>An armadillo is not a never-relocate species in Florida. A live-trapped nuisance armadillo must be handled within 24 hours: euthanised, released on-site, or released on a property within the same county of capture that is 40 acres or larger, with written permission from that landowner — public land only with the land manager's written permission. Our crew follows exactly that, and checks traps at intervals of no more than 24 hours. Work is done under FWC Rule 68A-9.010 with the property owner's authorization — Florida issues no nuisance-trapper license.</p>\n<h2>What a Florida Armadillo Job Includes</h2>\n<p>Inspection of burrows and digging routes, guided live trapping along travel lines and burrow mouths, lawful disposition under the rules above, burrow filling and screening once the animal is out, and fencing and grub advice to protect beds and lawn edges.</p>\n",
