      "leadParagraph": "<p>In Florida's heat, a dead animal in an attic, wall void, or crawlspace announces itself fast — a strong odour within days, flies at windows, and staining on ceilings. FWC does not collect carcasses for removal requests; its wildlife-health guidance notes a nuisance wildlife trapper can be contacted to remove carcasses at a cost, which is exactly this job. The usual animals are roof rats, opossums, raccoons, squirrels, and bats that died inside, plus yard carcasses after storms, road strikes, and predator kills.</p>",
      "extendedBody": "<h2>Finding the Carcass Is the Job</h2>\n<p>Odour is strongest near the source, flies gather at the light fixtures and windows closest to it, and the animal's entry route usually tells the story — a roof rat run, an opossum crawlspace, a raccoon soffit. Our crew traces the odour, uses inspection cameras in wall voids where needed, and removes the carcass with gloves and sealed containment, then treats the area and removes fouled insulation where the fluids have soaked in. Sealing the entry is part of the same visit's plan, or the next animal follows the same route.</p>\n<h2>Health Precautions in Florida</h2>\n<p>Raccoons, bats, and skunks are Florida rabies-vector species, so their carcasses are handled with protective equipment and never bare-handed, and any pet exposure to a carcass is a county health department question. A carcass of a protected or listed species — a gopher tortoise, a listed bird, an indigo snake — is not a pickup; it is a report to FWC, and our crew will tell you that on the call rather than removing something the law protects.</p>\n<h2>After Storms and in Vacant Homes</h2>\n<p>Hurricane season and long vacancies are Florida's peak dead-animal settings: displaced wildlife shelters in garages, lanais, and flooded structures, and animals that die in a closed, seasonally vacant house may not be found for weeks. Post-storm, give displaced live wildlife space, call FWC's alligator program about any threatening alligator, and call us about carcasses in or under the home. Work is done under FWC Rule 68A-9.010 with the property owner's authorization — Florida issues no nuisance-trapper license.</p>\n<h2>What a Florida Dead Animal Job Includes</h2>\n<p>Odour tracing and carcass location, sealed removal, disinfection and odour treatment of the affected area, removal of fouled insulation where needed, and inspection and sealing of the entry route so a live animal does not repeat the problem.</p>\n",
